# fleet — project-level configuration for healthsend
#
# The repository's own commands. FLEET_E2E_CMD is deliberately left empty:
# `pnpm e2e` drives a real send against Swarm and Arkiv and needs .env.local
# plus a signed-in passkey, none of which exist in a fresh worktree. A worker
# that ran it would report a failure belonging to this file rather than to its
# code. The recipient path is verified here, in the main checkout.

FLEET_TEST_CMD=pnpm verify:crypto
FLEET_LINT_CMD=pnpm lint
FLEET_TYPECHECK_CMD=pnpm exec tsc --noEmit
FLEET_BUILD_CMD=pnpm build
FLEET_DEV_CMD=pnpm dev
FLEET_START_CMD=pnpm start

# Concurrency. The cap is checked against a GLOBAL count of live `claude -p`
# sessions, and the codex engine runs through claude-code-proxy, so its workers
# count here too. Four is the ceiling the operator asked for: three anthropic
# (sonnet) plus one codex. Both engines need a threshold above 4 or the fourth
# launch is refused by whichever engine happens to be last.
#
# Note run-worker.sh's own comment: every session shares one proxy on
# 127.0.0.1:18765, and four concurrent is documented as run clean twice but
# "unproven". The failure mode is a rejected handshake, which the patched build
# retries internally.
FLEET_ANTHROPIC_MAX_CONCURRENT=8
FLEET_CODEX_MAX_CONCURRENT=8

# Raised to 5 for one adversarial review lane alongside four builders. Note
# run-worker.sh's own warning: every session shares one proxy on
# 127.0.0.1:18765 and four concurrent is already documented as "unproven".
# Five is further out. The failure mode is a rejected handshake, which the
# patched build retries internally; if reviews start stalling, this is the
# first number to put back.

# Verification ORDER matters here: `tsc --noEmit` fails with TS2304 on
# LayoutProps in a fresh worktree until `next build` has populated .next/types,
# because that type is generated by Next rather than written. Run the build
# before the typecheck. Found by the H-23 worker, which correctly proved it was
# pre-existing by stashing its own diff and re-running against a clean tree.

# Raised 2026-09-12 to carry 5 anthropic + 3 codex at once. Both thresholds read
# 8 because the cap is compared against a GLOBAL count of live `claude -p`
# sessions and codex runs through the same proxy — a per-engine number below the
# combined total would refuse whichever engine launched last. The 5/3 split is
# enforced by what the orchestrator dispatches, not by these numbers.
#
# This is well past what run-worker.sh documents: its own comment calls four
# "unproven" and settles on three, because every session shares one proxy on
# 127.0.0.1:18765. Eight is a deliberate overshoot at the operator's request.
# Symptoms to watch: transport retries in the run stderr, exit 29 (quota or rate
# limit), or workers stalling with no stream events. This is the first number to
# put back.
